BUILDING INSIDE THE HUMANESQUE ECOSYSTEM
A brief for anyone vibe-coding something and wanting it to count.
Paste this whole thing into your AI before you start. Read it yourself first.

WHAT THIS IS
Humanesque is a constituted realm: an epistemic, intellectual and
technological domain with its own contract, its own tooling and its own
provenance chain. Within it sit Zistgah (the habitat), ILM (language and
script), FAKIR (domains), PEDLER (physics/intent semantics) and a working
publishing and provenance stack that is already built, already licensed,
already minted and already public.

You are invited to build on it. You are not asked to submit to it.
Recognition is not submission; non-participation is not denial; disagreement
is not disrespect. Use what helps you, ignore the rest, keep your own
sovereignty over your own realm. That is a stated principle here, not a
courtesy — Sovereignty without Suzerainty.

STEP 0 — RETRIEVE BEFORE YOU BUILD
Do not design from what your AI remembers. Clone and read:
  github.com/zistgah/governance   → CONTRACT.md · CONTEXT.md · AGENTS.md
The master contract is twelve clauses plus a numbered clause series. The ones
that will bite you first:
  · Retrieval before assumption — discover, never hardcode. Org lists, repo
    lists, registries, endpoints: query the authoritative source, paginated.
  · No vendor lock-in — provider abstraction, bring-your-own-key, and at
    least one keyless or self-hostable path. Never name an AI vendor in
    shipped text; ship a placeholder and let the operator choose.
  · Nothing hardcoded, anywhere — handles, endpoints, review repos,
    callbacks. Shipping a default is fine. Shipping a constant is not.
  · Verify by execution — a test proves nothing until you have watched it
    fail. A definition is not a call. Assert the CALL SITE.
  · Never bluff — done means executed. Written-but-unrun says so. Partial is
    reported as partial; four of ten is never progress on ten. Retrieved
    beats remembered. A 404 at one path is reported as exactly that.
  · Gated irreversibility — anything public and permanent (a push, a DOI, a
    deletion) happens only when a human types an assertion word plus a
    reason. That typed line IS the intent record.
  · Build context before you touch a repo — a canonical mirror is read, a
    working copy is edited. Never edit canonical in place.
  · No dead stops, no donkey work — a script that can do the thing does the
    thing. Printing "now run this" to your operator is the failure, not the
    fallback.
A per-repo CONTRACT.md may ADD constraints. It may never relax them.

STEP 1 — DO NOT REBUILD WHAT EXISTS
All of this is live, public, licensed and DOI'd. Fork it, drive it, extend
it — but do not reinvent it and then tell us it is new.

  · zistgah/kitab — config-driven publishing template. A book is a config
    plus content JSON plus figures; the plumbing is never touched. Three
    readers (paginated / scroll / liquid), figures that explain themselves,
    read aloud and hand off to an AI, inline exercises, issue-based comments.
  · zistgah/matba — the press. Local app + web UI + CLI + Colab notebook,
    stdlib only, no dependencies. Automates the whole seed → doctor → seal →
    push → mint path. Carries the composing room and THE CYCLER.
    https://zistgah.github.io/matba/ · DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21948734
  · zistgah/dome — the shared virtual dome as a config-driven library. Mount
    your own display inside it; worlds are config, never forks.
  · zistgah/mez — the desk. Where the tools sit when you are working.
  · project-ilm/misty-doi — `pipx install misty-doi`, CLI `misty`. Metadata,
    packaging, OpenTimestamps, Zenodo minting, concept-vs-version DOIs, and
    MASI: seven scholarly tracks (ethics, prereg, paper, journal, conference,
    chapter, patent) with gates that bite. DOI 10.5281/zenodo.20719388
  · project-ilm/tok-doi — atomic provenance. Register cryptographic proof at
    arbitrary granularity: artifact → SHA-256 → OpenTimestamps → registry.
    Browser-first, no backend, keyless. DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21402745
  · zistgah/poie — the Proclamation of Individual Equity, the root
    constitutional artifact. DOI 10.5281/zenodo.21397274

THE CYCLER — the pattern worth stealing
Most people do not have an API budget and should not have to. The cycler is a
one-button clipboard loop: press once to copy the prompt, go to whichever AI
you use, come back and press once more — it reads the clipboard, routes the
answer to the step it belongs to by SHAPE, files it, and copies the next
prompt. No form filling. No provider preconfigured. The AI source table ships
EMPTY and the operator adds their own.
Two rules that came out of building it, and that you should adopt:
  · Endpoints are shapes, not providers. Ship the shape, never the brand.
  · Filesystem visibility is not provenance. When files appear in a folder,
    say "3 new files are visible" — never "3 files your AI generated".

STEP 2 — BUILD IT PROPERLY
  · Static first. GitHub Pages, no backend, no server-side secret. If it
    needs a server to be useful, it is not finished.
  · No browser storage as the source of truth. State in memory, truth in
    exported files.
  · Scripts: idempotent on rerun, run from the invoking directory, never
    /tmp, tokens read by PATH and never inlined or passed as flags.
  · Patch by exact anchor and abort if the anchor moved. Never regenerate a
    whole file because it was easier.
  · Report a gap. Never fill one with an invention.

STEP 3 — SEAL AND MINT
  1. SHA-256 manifest over the tracked tree. Receipts written and stamped
     FIRST, then the manifest, then stamp the manifest — a manifest cannot
     contain the hash of its own proof.
  2. OpenTimestamps every receipt: `misty ots stamp <path>`, one path per
     invocation. Absent tool = step declared skipped, never faked.
  3. Mint: `misty publish -m <metadata> -f <artifact> <figures...>`, then
     RECORD THE DOI BACK into config, metadata, CITATION.cff and the README
     badge. A minted DOI that is not written back into the repo is a defect,
     and it is the most common one.
  4. Version, do not duplicate: `misty newversion` under the concept DOI. Two
     records of the same work under two concept DOIs cannot be merged
     afterwards without begging the registry.

LICENSING — decide before the first commit
  · Code: GPL-3.0-or-later unless a repo states otherwise. SPDX header in
    every file. Ship the full licence text unmodified.
  · Docs, schemas, sample content: CC BY-SA 4.0.
  · Constitutional artifacts are CC BY-ND — quote them, cite them, do not
    rewrite them and call the result the same instrument.
  · Your own work carries YOUR copyright line and YOUR name. Attribution
    runs both ways; nobody here wants your authorship.
  · If you intend to contribute upstream rather than fork, ask about the
    inbound contribution terms first — a dual-licensing policy is in
    preparation and is not yet published. Do not assume either way.

THE HONOUR SYSTEM
Copyleft is enforceable in principle and almost never enforced in practice at
this scale, which means what you do with it is a statement about you, not
about the risk you are running. Credit upstream when nobody would catch you.
Say what you forked in the commit message, not only in a README nobody opens.
Do not strip headers. Do not re-badge someone's architecture as inspiration.
Cite the DOI when you build on the work — that is what it exists for. And
when someone builds on yours, take it as the compliment it is.

Mint your own work. Attribute what is not yours. That is the whole rule.

HOW TO COME IN
  · Build the thing. Publish it. Get it a DOI of your own.
  · Open an issue on whichever repo you built against — say what you took,
    what you changed, what broke.
  · The strongest way in is a COUNTER-OBSERVATION: find where the contract,
    the tooling or the argument is wrong, and say so with evidence.
    Agreement earns nothing here. A defect found earns standing. Nothing is
    ever deleted as a sanction and nobody is characterised as defective for
    walking away.
  · Sign the Proclamation of Individual Equity if it speaks to you, and do
    not if it does not. Both are answers.

WHAT TO SEND BACK
Reply with:
  (i)   your repo URL
  (ii)  your licence files and your copyright line
  (iii) your DOI, or why you have decided not to mint
  (iv)  one thing in this brief you think is wrong

TELL YOUR AI THIS TOO
Read before you assert. Do not substitute a familiar concept for the one you
were actually given, then reason well about the wrong thing. Do not reach for
"what accepted category does this reduce to" before "what ontology makes this
coherent". Reconstruct faithfully, then critique with evidence, not tone.
Understanding is not agreement — and disagreement after accurate
reconstruction is welcome.
